            "id": "PR_kwDOErHI988AAAABI1lRPA",
            "number": 1601,
            "title": "fix(body): match form content type case-insensitively in `readBody`",
            "body": "`readBody()` decides whether to parse the body as url-encoded with a case-sensitive `startsWith(\"application/x-www-form-urlencoded\")`. Media types are case-insensitive (RFC 9110, section 8.3.1: \"The type and subtype tokens are case-insensitive.\"), so a request with a differently cased type falls through to `JSON.parse` and gets a 400:\n\n```js\napp.post(\"/\", (event) => readBody(event));\n```\n\n```\n$ curl -s localhost:3000 -H 'content-type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded' -d 'a=1&b=2'\n{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}\n$ curl -s localhost:3000 -H 'content-type: Application/x-www-form-urlencoded' -d 'a=1&b=2'\n{\"status\":400,\"statusText\":\"Bad Request\",\"message\":\"Invalid JSON body\"}\n```\n\n`readBody(event, { type: \"formData\" })` already accepts the same request, since `Request.formData()` parses the header case-insensitively, so the default path was the odd one out.\n\nThe fix lowercases the header before the check. The new test sends two differently cased form content types (one with a `charset` parameter) through the web and node matrix; without the source change both fail with `expected { status: 400, ... } to deeply equal { field: 'value', ... }`.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * URL-encoded form bodies are now parsed correctly when the `Content-Type` header uses uppercase or mixed-case letters, including with a charset parameter.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
